# Scheduled work for the inventory app. OPERATIONS.md §5 marks both jobs required.
#
# Install as the user that owns the app — NOT root. The app's files, the backup
# directory and these jobs should all belong to one unprivileged user:
#
#   crontab -u inventory deploy/crontab
#   crontab -l -u inventory          # confirm it took
#
# PLACEHOLDERS: /srv/inventory (twice per line) and the MAILTO address.

# Cron runs with a near-empty PATH — /usr/bin is not on it by default, so `npm`
# is not found and the job fails silently. This line is why these entries work.
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
SHELL=/bin/bash

# Cron mails stdout to the crontab owner by default, i.e. nowhere anybody reads.
# The sweep reporting drift is the single most important message this system can
# send; it must reach a person.
MAILTO=ops@example.com

# The `cd` is load-bearing. Both scripts call `import 'dotenv/config'`, which
# resolves .env relative to the working directory. Run them from anywhere else
# and they start with no DATABASE_URL and fail on connect.

# --- The nightly sweep -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Compares stock_levels against the ledger, and expires batches whose date has
# passed. The projection agreeing with the ledger is this system's central claim
# about itself; until this runs on a schedule, the only thing that has ever
# checked it is a test on a throwaway database.
#
# 02:10, before the backup, so a dump captures a swept database.
10 2 * * *  cd /srv/inventory && npm run sweep >> /var/log/inventory/sweep.log 2>&1

# Demo mode is a product feature with its own database, so it drifts too. Later
# and separate: a failure here must not stop the live sweep from having run.
25 2 * * *  cd /srv/inventory && npm run sweep -- demo >> /var/log/inventory/sweep-demo.log 2>&1

# --- Backups -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# mysqldump --single-transaction, password passed via MYSQL_PWD rather than argv
# so it never appears in `ps`. Writes to BACKUP_DIR from .env.
40 2 * * *  cd /srv/inventory && npm run backup >> /var/log/inventory/backup.log 2>&1

# --- Not scheduled, on purpose -----------------------------------------------
#
# `npm run restore:verify` does a full round trip into a scratch database and
# compares table sets, row counts and CHECKSUM TABLE. It is the only thing that
# turns "we have backups" from a hypothesis into a fact.
#
# It is not in cron because it should be run by a person who then reads the
# output — an automated drill whose result nobody looks at proves nothing. Run it
# once before real data exists, and quarterly after. OPERATIONS.md §6.
#
# Also consider: BACKUP_DIR fills up. Nothing here prunes it, because how long to
# keep dumps of a warehouse's stock history is a retention decision, not a
# default. Decide it, then add the find -mtime line here.
